## The `RunDSL` primitive

`Boukensha::RunDSL` → `boukensha/run_dsl.py`, `RunDSL` class. The object `self` becomes inside a
`Boukensha.run` block via `instance_eval`; exposes exactly one method, `tool`, keeping the DSL
surface intentionally small.

| Ruby | Python | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `initialize(registry)` | `__init__(self, registry: Registry) -> None` | store as `self._registry` |
| `tool(name, description:, parameters: {}, &block)` calls `@registry.tool(name, description:, parameters:, &block)` | `def tool(self, name: str, *, description: str, parameters: dict[str, Any] \| None = None, block: Callable[..., Any]) -> Tool` | forwards straight to `self._registry.tool(name, description=description, parameters=parameters, block=block)` — `Registry.tool` already normalizes `parameters or {}` internally (established `02_the_registry`), so `RunDSL.tool` doesn't need its own `parameters or {}` |

Python has no block-with-implicit-`self`-swap (`instance_eval`) — see the "Translating the
`instance_eval` block" open question below for how `Boukensha.run`'s block param itself is
represented.

## `Boukensha.run`

`Boukensha.run` → a module-level `run` function in `boukensha/__init__.py` (Ruby hangs this
directly off the `Boukensha` module, same as `06_the_logger`'s global-state functions; unlike
those, there's no name collision to work around here — no `boukensha/run.py` submodule exists).

| Ruby | Python | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `self.run(task:, system: nil, model: nil, backend: nil, api_key: nil, ollama_host: "http://localhost:11434", log: nil, max_output_tokens: nil, &block)` | `def run(*, task: str, system: str \| None = None, model: str \| None = None, backend: str \| None = None, api_key: str \| None = None, ollama_host: str = "http://localhost:11434", log: str \| Path \| None = None, max_output_tokens: int \| None = None, configure: Callable[[RunDSL], None] \| None = None) -> str` | see the "block" naming open question — `configure` is this plan's recommendation, not settled |
| `cfg = config` (the global memoized accessor) | `cfg = get_config()` | |
| `task_class = Tasks::Player` | `task_class = Player` | hardcoded, matches Ruby exactly — no task-selection param exists in this step |
| `task_settings = cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name)` | `task_settings = cfg.tasks(task_class.task_name())` | |
| `system \|\|= task_class.system_prompt(task_settings, user_prompts_dir: cfg.user_prompts_dir, default_prompts_dir: Config::PROMPTS_DIR)` | `system = system or task_class.system_prompt(task_settings, user_prompts_dir=cfg.user_prompts_dir, default_prompts_dir=Config.PROMPTS_DIR)` | |
| `model \|\|= task_class.model(task_settings)` | `model = model or task_class.model(task_settings)` | |
| `backend \|\|= task_class.provider(task_settings).to_sym` | `backend = backend or task_class.provider(task_settings)` | plain `str`, no symbol/string duality to port (established since `00_config`) |
| `api_key \|\|=` case on backend → `ENV["..._API_KEY"]` (no branch for `:ollama`) | `api_key = api_key or {"anthropic": os.environ.get("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"), "openai": os.environ.get("OPENAI_API_KEY"), "gemini": os.environ.get("GEMINI_API_KEY"), "ollama_cloud": os.environ.get("OLLAMA_API_KEY")}.get(backend)` | uses `ENV["KEY"]`/`os.environ.get`, **not** `.fetch`/`os.environ[...]` — silently `None` if the env var is unset, unlike `examples/example.rb`'s old `ENV.fetch(...)` (which raised). Port this exactly; it's a real, deliberate loosening in Ruby's own source, not something to "fix" back to a raising fetch |
| `ctx = Context.new(task: task_class, system: system)` / `registry = Registry.new(ctx)` | direct port | |
| `RunDSL.new(registry).instance_eval(&block) if block` | `if configure is not None: configure(RunDSL(registry))` | see open question below |
| `be = case backend ... end` (5 branches + `else raise ArgumentError, "Unknown backend ..."`) | `match backend: case "anthropic": ... case _: raise ValueError(f"Unknown backend {backend!r}. Use 'anthropic', 'openai', 'gemini', 'ollama', or 'ollama_cloud'.")` | mirrors `examples/example.py`'s existing `match provider:` block; Ruby `ArgumentError` → Python `ValueError`, matching `tasks/base.py`'s existing convention |
| `builder = PromptBuilder.new(ctx, be)` / `client = Client.new(builder)` | direct port | |
| `effective_max_iterations = task_class.max_iterations(task_settings)` | direct port | no `max_iterations:` param exists on `Boukensha.run` at all — always task-settings-derived |
| `effective_max_output_tokens = max_output_tokens \|\| task_class.max_output_tokens(task_settings)` | `effective_max_output_tokens = max_output_tokens or task_class.max_output_tokens(task_settings)` | |
| `logger = Logger.new(log: log, snapshot: {task:, max_iterations:, max_output_tokens:, model:, provider: backend})` | `logger = Logger(log=log, snapshot={"task": task_class.task_name(), "max_iterations": effective_max_iterations, "max_output_tokens": effective_max_output_tokens, "model": model, "provider": backend})` | |
| `agent = Agent.new(context:, registry:, builder:, client:, logger:, task_settings:, max_iterations: effective_max_iterations, max_output_tokens: effective_max_output_tokens)` | direct port | |
| `ctx.add_message(:user, task)` | `ctx.add_message("user", task)` | |
| `agent.run` (return value) `ensure logger&.close` | `try: ... return agent.run() finally: if logger is not None: logger.close()` | `logger` must be pre-declared `logger: Logger | None = None` before the `try`, since Ruby's `ensure` also runs (with `logger` still `nil`) if an exception is raised *before* the `Logger.new` line executes (e.g. an unknown backend) |

